A sieve makes all prime checks quick. I first mark the prime numbers up to `n`, then scan `p` from 2 through `n // 2`. If both `p` and `n - p` are marked, the answer is `True`.

Dry run for `n = 10`: the sieve marks `2, 3, 5, 7` as prime. The pair `3` and `7` is found, so the method returns `True`.

```python
class Solution:
    def is_sum_of_two_primes(self, n):
        if n < 4:
            return False

        prime = [True] * (n + 1)
        prime[0] = False
        prime[1] = False

        limit = int(n ** 0.5)
        for value in range(2, limit + 1):
            if prime[value]:
                for multiple in range(value * value, n + 1, value):
                    prime[multiple] = False

        for first in range(2, n // 2 + 1):
            if prime[first] and prime[n - first]:
                return True
        return False
```

Time complexity: `O(n log log n)` for the sieve and `O(n)` for the scan.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

I check every possible first prime `p` and test whether `n - p` is also prime. The first valid pair is enough to return `true`.

The small cases matter here: values below 4 cannot be written as a sum of two primes. For example, with `n = 10`, the checks eventually find `3 + 7`.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    bool isPrime(int value) {
        if (value < 2) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int divisor = 2; 1LL * divisor * divisor <= value; ++divisor) {
            if (value % divisor == 0) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

public:
    bool isSumOfTwoPrimes(int n) {
        if (n < 4) {
            return false;
        }

        for (int first = 2; first <= n / 2; ++first) {
            if (isPrime(first) && isPrime(n - first)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n sqrt(n))` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This keeps the check simple and skips many impossible candidates. Two primes can add to an odd number only when one of them is 2, so odd `n` is handled directly with `2` and `n - 2`. For even `n`, I test odd candidates and their complements.

The edge cases are `n = 1`, `n = 2`, and `n = 3`, all of which return `false`. The smallest possible answer is `4 = 2 + 2`.

```go
func isSumOfTwoPrimes(n int) bool {
	if n < 4 {
		return false
	}

	if n%2 == 1 {
		return isPrime(n - 2)
	}

	for first := 3; first <= n/2; first += 2 {
		if isPrime(first) && isPrime(n-first) {
			return true
		}
	}

	return true && isPrime(2) && isPrime(n-2)
}

func isPrime(value int) bool {
	if value < 2 {
		return false
	}
	if value == 2 {
		return true
	}
	if value%2 == 0 {
		return false
	}

	for divisor := 3; divisor <= value/divisor; divisor += 2 {
		if value%divisor == 0 {
			return false
		}
	}
	return true
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n sqrt(n))` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

I generate the primes first, then use two pointers on the sorted list. If the current sum is too small, the left pointer moves forward. If it is too large, the right pointer moves backward.

For `n = 10`, the prime list is `[2, 3, 5, 7]`. The pointers try `2 + 7 = 9`, then move left to get `3 + 7 = 10`.

```javascript
class Solution {
    isSumOfTwoPrimes(n) {
        if (n < 4) {
            return false;
        }

        const prime = new Array(n + 1).fill(true);
        prime[0] = false;
        prime[1] = false;

        for (let value = 2; value * value <= n; value++) {
            if (prime[value]) {
                for (let multiple = value * value; multiple <= n; multiple += value) {
                    prime[multiple] = false;
                }
            }
        }

        const primes = [];
        for (let value = 2; value <= n; value++) {
            if (prime[value]) {
                primes.push(value);
            }
        }

        let left = 0;
        let right = primes.length - 1;
        while (left <= right) {
            const sum = primes[left] + primes[right];
            if (sum === n) {
                return true;
            }
            if (sum < n) {
                left++;
            } else {
                right--;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log log n)` for the sieve and `O(n)` for the two-pointer scan.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

This version builds a smallest-prime-factor table. A number is prime when its smallest factor is itself. After building the table, every candidate can be checked in constant time.

The loop only goes to `n / 2`, because the pair `(p, n - p)` would otherwise be checked again in reverse. For `n < 4`, no pair of primes is possible.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isSumOfTwoPrimes(int n) {
        if (n < 4) {
            return false;
        }

        int[] smallestFactor = new int[n + 1];
        for (int value = 2; value <= n; value++) {
            if (smallestFactor[value] == 0) {
                smallestFactor[value] = value;
                if ((long) value * value <= n) {
                    for (int multiple = value * value; multiple <= n; multiple += value) {
                        if (smallestFactor[multiple] == 0) {
                            smallestFactor[multiple] = value;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        for (int first = 2; first <= n / 2; first++) {
            int second = n - first;
            if (smallestFactor[first] == first && smallestFactor[second] == second) {
                return true;
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log log n)` to build the table, followed by `O(n)` checking.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

## Intuition

We need to determine whether `n` can be represented as the sum of two prime numbers.

If we choose one number as `i`, the other number is automatically `n - i`. Therefore, we can check every possible `i` and see whether both `i` and `n - i` are prime.

## Approach

1. Iterate from `2` to `n - 1`.
2. For every `i`, check whether `i` is prime.
3. Check whether `n - i` is also prime.
4. If both are prime, return `true`.
5. If no such pair is found, return `false`.

To check whether a number is prime, we only test divisors up to its square root. If no divisor is found, the number is prime.

## Solution

```java
class Solution {

    public boolean isSumOfTwoPrimes(int n) {

        for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
            if (isprime(i) && isprime(n - i))
                return true;
        }

        return false;
    }

    public static boolean isprime(int n) {

        if (n < 2) return false;

        for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
            if (n % i == 0)
                return false;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

## Complexity

**Time Complexity:**
`O(n√n)` in the worst case, since we check up to `n` possible values and each primality check takes up to `O(√n)` time.

**Space Complexity:**
`O(1)`, since we use only a constant amount of extra space.
